{"records":[{"id":"vkxsbyc9js","title":"Original western blot and gel images_Manuscript: Endogenous anti-tumorigenic nitro-fatty acids inhibit the ubiquitin-proteasome system by directly targeting the 26S proteasome","description":"Original western blot and gel images of the manuscript: Endogenous anti-tumorigenic nitro-fatty acids\ninhibit the ubiquitin-proteasome system by directly targeting the 26S proteasome (published in Cell Chem Biol; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chembiol.2023.06.017)\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-17T04:23:53.707Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vkxsbyc9js","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vkxsbyc9js"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Roos","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteasome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jjxy3f462r","title":"Intrinsic Bias of the Genetic Code Shapes the Folding and Stability Landscapes of Microproteins. Guo et al","description":"This dataset contains the raw immunofluorescence microscopy images, uncropped Western blot data, predicted protein structures, and analysis code corresponding to all experiments and analyses presented in the manuscript.\n\nThe immunofluorescence microscopy images were generated to investigate the intracellular stability and degradation pathways of microproteins translated from regulatory upstream open reading frames (uORFs). The working hypothesis is that microproteins encoded by these regulatory uORFs are selectively degraded through cellular protein quality control pathways, including the ubiquitin–proteasome system or the autophagy–lysosome pathway.\n\nTo test this hypothesis, cells expressing HA-tagged microproteins derived from regulatory uORFs were treated with the proteasome inhibitor MG132 or the lysosome inhibitor Chloroquine (CQ). These pharmacological perturbations were used to determine whether inhibition of specific degradation pathways results in the accumulation of the microproteins.\n\nAfter immunofluorescence staining, images were acquired using an Olympus FV3000 laser scanning confocal microscope equipped with a 60 × oil immersion objective. Z-stack images were collected with a step size of 0.7 μm. Raw fluorescence images were processed and quantified using ImageJ. The data can be used to compare fluorescence intensity changes between different treatment conditions and to evaluate the stability of microproteins under perturbation of protein degradation pathways. The results indicate that microproteins encoded by these regulatory uORFs are primarily degraded through the ubiquitin–proteasome system, as evidenced by their accumulation following treatment with the proteasome inhibitor MG132, whereas inhibition of lysosomal degradation using CQ shows comparatively limited effects.\n\nThe second part of the dataset provides the uncropped source data for all Western blot experiments presented in the manuscript. These images correspond to the Western blot figures shown in both the main figures and supplementary figures and are provided to ensure transparency and allow independent assessment and verification of the reported results.\n\nThe third part of the dataset contains predicted protein structures of all microproteins analyzed in this study, organized within a single directory. Two compressed archives are provided, corresponding to structures generated using ESMFold and AlphaFold2, respectively.\n\nThe fourth part of the dataset provides the code used in this study, including scripts and notebooks for structural analysis and ESM-2–based modeling of microprotein stability, supporting data processing, statistical analysis, and figure generation.\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-11T16:13:16.574Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jjxy3f462r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jjxy3f462r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Bioinformatics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rv8f9bp5b2","title":"Timer-based proteomic profiling of the ubiquitin-proteasome system reveals a substrate receptor of the GID ubiquitin ligase","description":"Unprocessed immunoblot images","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-14T13:34:37.803Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rv8f9bp5b2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rv8f9bp5b2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anton","last_name":"Khmelinskii","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7n36xfmfj9","title":"Inhibition of proteolytic and ATPase activities of the proteasome by the BTK inhibitor CGI-1746","description":"Bruton’s tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitor, ibrutinib, has been shown to synergize in vitro with proteasome inhibitors (PIs) in reducing the viability of cells derived from B-cell malignancies, but the mechanism is not known. We report here that an off-target effect of ibrutinib causes synergy because not all BTK inhibitors exhibited the synergistic effect, and those that synergized did so even in cells that do not express BTK. The allosteric BTK inhibitor CGI-1746 showed the strongest synergy. Co-treatment of cells with CGI-1746 increased PI-induced accumulation of ubiquitin conjugates, expression of heat shock proteins and NOXA, and decreased a ratio of reduced to oxidized glutathione. CGI-1746, but not other BTK inhibitors inhibited ATPase activity and all three peptidase activities of the 26S proteasomes. The effect demonstrates a conceptually novel mode of proteasome inhibition that may aid the development of more potent proteasome inhibitors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-07T14:50:38.999Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7n36xfmfj9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7n36xfmfj9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Olasubomi","last_name":"Akintola","orcid":"0000-0002-7414-6630"},{"first_name":"Mitchell","last_name":"Patterson","orcid":"0000-0003-4210-9074"},{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Smith","orcid":null},{"first_name":"George","last_name":"De Martino","orcid":"0000-0001-8718-783X"},{"first_name":"Amit","last_name":"Mitra","orcid":"0000-0003-4046-7070"},{"first_name":"Alexei","last_name":"Kisselev","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Proteasome","Enzymatic Activity","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w99dh6rymx","title":"RNF126-mediated reubiquitination is required for proteasomal degradation of p97-extracted membrane proteins. Hu et al","description":"Valosin-containing protein (VCP)/p97 is an AAA-ATPase that extracts poly-ubiquitinated substrates from multimeric macromolecular complexes and biological membranes for proteasomal degradation. During p97-mediated extraction, the substrate is largely deubiquitinated as it is threaded through the p97 central pore. How p97-extracted substrates are targeted to the proteasome with few or no ubiquitins is unknown. Here, we report that p97-extracted membrane proteins undergo a second round of ubiquitination catalyzed by the cytosolic ubiquitin ligase RNF126. RNF126 interacts with transmembrane domain-specific chaperone BAG6, which captures p97-liberated substrates. RNF126 depletion in cells diminishes the ubiquitination of extracted membrane proteins, slows down their turnover, and dramatically stabilizes otherwise transient intermediates in the cytosol. We reconstitute the reubiquitination of a p97-extracted, misfolded multi-spanning membrane protein with purified factors. Our results demonstrate that p97-extracted substrates need to rapidly engage ubiquitin ligase/chaperone pairs that rebuild the ubiquitin signal for proteasome targeting to prevent harmful accumulation of unfolded intermediates. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-06-12T15:36:45.32Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w99dh6rymx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w99dh6rymx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zairong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xianyan","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Linhan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuancai","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiqiang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Congcong","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yaoyang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhiyuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j99yg4nttw","title":"Streptococcus suis serotype 2 evades autophagic clearance via mitochondrial ROS-induced LMP and ATP6V1B1 downregulation","description":"Streptococcus suis serotype 2 (SS2) is an emerging zoonotic pathogen that causes severe infections with high mortality, yet its immune evasion mechanisms remain incompletely understood. Here, we found that SS2 infection induced autophagic flux blockade, as evidenced by accumulation of LC3-II and p62 and formation of non functional autolysosomes. Mechanistic studies revealed that SS2 infection caused mitochondrial damage, characterized by ROS burst, membrane potential loss, and ATP depletion. Excessive ROS triggered lysosomal membrane permeabilization (LMP) and downregulated ATP6V1B1, synergistically impairing lysosomal acidification, blocking autophagic flux, and promoting SS2 survival. Overexpression of ATP6V1B1 restored lysosomal pH, Cathepsin D activity, and autophagic flux, and reduced bacterial survival. SS2 also disrupted the host compensatory response by accelerating TFEB degradation via the ubiquitin‑proteasome pathway. In vivo, inhibition of lysosomal acidification exacerbates SS2‑induced mortality, organ bacterial loads and tissue damage, whereas ATP6V1B1 overexpression confers protection. This study reveals the molecular mechanism by which SS2 hijacks the host autophagic system, providing a theoretical foundation for developing host-directed anti-SS2 strategies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-06T13:56:00.648Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j99yg4nttw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j99yg4nttw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fei","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuzhi","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pei","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianda","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luogang","last_name":"Ding","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yulin","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhi","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenbo","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiang","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiaqiang","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bacteria","Pathogenesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65k3y4smr5","title":"ATG5 attenuates inflammatory signaling in embryonic stem cells to control differentiation","description":"Attenuated inflammatory responses to both inflammation-related cytokines and infectious agents are recognized as a unique property of embryonic stem cells (ESCs). Although an increasing number of studies have reported this attenuation, the underlying molecular mechanisms are still unclear. Moreover, whether the attenuated inflammatory status is involved in ESC differentiation is completely unknown. Here, we found that the autophagy-related protein ATG5 is essential for both the attenuated inflammatory response and the differentiation of ESCs, and that the attenuation of inflammatory signaling is required for ESC differentiation. Mechanistically, ATG5 promotes 26S proteasome-mediated degradation of the SCF (SKP1-cullin-1-F-box-protein) E3 ubiquitin ligase β-TrCP1 (beta-transducin repeat containing E3 ubiquitin protein ligase 1), which is a well-known positive regulator of NF-κB signaling and the inflammatory response. Another SCF E3 ubiquitin ligase, FBXW7 (F-box and WD repeat domain containing 7), which is recruited by ATG5, was found to be the E3 ubiquitin ligase responsible for the ubiquitination and degradation of β-TrCP1. Moreover, the differentiation defects observed in ATG5-depleted ESCs are due to the accumulation of β-TrCP1 and the related hyperactivation of NF-κB signaling, as loss of β-TrCP1 and inhibition of NF-κB signaling rescued the differentiation defects in ATG5-depleted ESCs. Therefore, this study reveals a previously uncharacterized mechanism employed by ESCs to maintain the inflammatory response in an attenuated state and further provides novel insight into the biological roles of ATG5, especially in the control of the inflammatory response and differentiation of ESCs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-22T08:57:47.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65k3y4smr5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65k3y4smr5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bo-Wen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying-Cui","last_name":"Wan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bei-Bei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nan","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian-Ru","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Fan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jia-Ni","last_name":"Shang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rui","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen-Ge","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yan","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Su","last_name":"Chen","orcid":"0000-0001-8804-8989"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"ZR2021QC181"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"222300420028"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82273067"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81972650"}],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g9tpsdctt4","title":"20S and 26S Proteasome-binding proteins of the rabbit brain: a proteomic dataset ","description":"Fractions of 26S and 20S proteasomes isolated from the rabbit brain by the method of salt fractionation (salt-induced precipitation) contain intrinsic proteasome proteins responsible for assembly of the core particle and regulatory particle of proteasome and also proteasome-binding proteins. These proteasome-binding proteins include components of the ubiquitin-proteasome system, some ubiquitinated proteins, as well as cytoskeleton components, protective proteins, regulators of gene expression, cell division, and differentiation, and multifunctional proteins (mainly, glycolytic enzymes: glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH), aldolase, pyruvate kinase, etc.). The multifunctional proteins are also known as “moonlighting proteins” are involved in various (regulatory) processes in the cell and this suggests that they represent important components of the proteasome interactome rather than contaminants of the 26S and 20S proteasome fractions.\n\nData obtained by high-resolution mass spectrometry in Ubiquitine-tag triggered MS3-mode with synchronous fragment ions selection, and nano-flow liquid chromatography (see \"Steps to reproduce for details). The presented dataset comprised of two subsets: (1) Data of proteomes obtained for each animal under the study; these data are in Excel format and contain necessary information about proteins, peptides, fragments, and their abundance, and other related meta-data. Data contained specific modifications identified in peptides by the PSMs. (2)  Combined data for the complete cohort of animals with protein distribution.\n\nBoth subsets also contain information about semi-quantitative protein representation estimated by the NSAF algorithm in the Search GUI/Peptide Shaker search engine.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-01T12:12:39.776Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g9tpsdctt4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g9tpsdctt4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexey","last_name":"Medvedev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Olga","last_name":"Buneeva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arthur T","last_name":"Kopylov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteasome","Brain","Rabbit","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j9gznvffcd","title":"Raw data - Feedback inhibition of cAMP effector signaling by a chaperone-assisted ubiquitin system","description":"Research data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-15T12:00:40.328Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j9gznvffcd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j9gznvffcd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lucio","last_name":"Annunziato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rjtsyp7vv3","title":"Cockayne’s syndrome A and B proteins regulate transcription arrest after genotoxic stress by promoting ATF3 degradation","description":"This data represent the original western blots covering the in vitro data and original ChIP assays with % to input data from the \"Cockayne syndrome A and B proteins regulate the timing of transcription arrest upon genotoxic stress through a ubiquitin/proteasome degradation process\" publication.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2017-11-15T15:37:56.366Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rjtsyp7vv3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rjtsyp7vv3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexey","last_name":"Epanchintsev","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Federico","last_name":"Costanzo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marc-Alexander","last_name":"Rauschendorf","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuela","last_name":"Caputo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luca","last_name":"Proietti De Santis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tao","last_name":"Ye","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lise-Marie","last_name":"Donnio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frédéric ","last_name":"Coin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vincent","last_name":"Laugel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jean-Marc","last_name":"Egly","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Paper (Organic Polymer)"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":135974,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":154,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":135974,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":135402,"restricted":65,"embargoed":507},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2321},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1411},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":657},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":376},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":331},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":280}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":133335},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1904},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":134},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":76},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":914},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":857},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":510},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}